index 0000000..a97da12
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Doc/library/macros.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+:mod:`!macros` --- Syntactic macros
+===================================
+
+.. module:: macros
+   :synopsis: Support for syntactic macros (PEP 638).
+
+**Source code:** :source:`Lib/macros.py`
+
+--------------
+
+This module supports *syntactic macros* as specified by :pep:`638`.
+A macro is a function that is called by the compiler and transforms
+a part of the abstract syntax tree of the program.
+
+Using macros
+------------
+
+The name of a macro is followed by an exclamation mark. There are three
+kinds of macros::
+
+    from! mymacros import unless, traced, dbg
+
+    unless! x > 10:            # a statement macro, here with an indented body
+        print("small")
+
+    traced!                    # a sibling macro: it applies to the statement
+    def f():                   # that follows it in the same block
+        ...
+
+    y = dbg!(x + 1)            # an expression macro
+
+A statement that starts with the name of a macro is always a macro statement.
+The general forms are:
+
+.. productionlist:: python-grammar
+   macro_stmt: MACRO_NAME [`expression_list`] ["import" `identifier`] ["as" `identifier`] [":" `suite`]
+   macro_expr: MACRO_NAME "(" [`expression_list`] ")"
+
+The statements of the body of a macro can also be "assignments" to arbitrary
+expressions, such as ``"red" = 1``: the body is only given a meaning by the
+macro.
+
+Macros must be imported before they are used, with the predefined macros
+``import!`` and ``from!``::
+
+    from! module import name [as othername]
+    import! module.name [as othername]
+
+The import is done *at compile time*, and the macro is only defined in the
+scope (module, class or function, including nested scopes) that contains the
+import, from the import onwards. As the macros of a module are needed to
+compile it, a macro cannot be used in the module that defines it.
+
+The compiler records the macros that a module imports in the module
+variable ``__macros__``. It is used to compile the module again, instead of
+using its cached bytecode, when the version of one of these macros changes.
+
+Defining macros
+---------------
+
+A *macro processor* is a tuple ``(func, kind, version, additional_names)``:
+
+* *func* is called with the :class:`ast.MacroStmt` or :class:`ast.MacroExpr`
+  node of the macro, and returns the tree that replaces it. A statement
+  macro can also return a list of statements, or ``None`` to remove the
+  statement. The result can contain other macros, which are expanded in turn.
+* *kind* is :data:`STMT_MACRO`, :data:`SIBLING_MACRO` or :data:`EXPR_MACRO`.
+* *version* is an integer that must be changed when the result of the macro
+  changes.
+* *additional_names* is a tuple with the names of the additional parts of
+  a statement macro. If the macro statement is followed by macro statements
+  with these names, in this order, their nodes are passed to *func* as
+  additional arguments (``None`` for the parts that are absent)::
+
+      # (func, STMT_MACRO, 1, ("finally",)); func(node, finally_node)
+      try_!:
+          body
+      finally!:
+          closing
+
+Nodes created by a macro processor without a location get the location
+of the macro.
+
+.. data:: STMT_MACRO
+          SIBLING_MACRO
+          EXPR_MACRO
+
+   The kinds of macros.
+
+.. decorator:: macro_processor(kind, version, *additional_names)
+
+   Mark a function as a macro processor::
+
+      import ast
+      import macros
+
+      @macros.macro_processor(macros.STMT_MACRO, 1)
+      def unless(node):
+          test = ast.UnaryOp(ast.Not(), node.args)
+          return ast.If(test, node.body, [])
+
+.. function:: new_name(node, variable=None)
+
+   Return a name for a variable that is created by a macro, following the
+   scheme recommended by :pep:`638`: ``$$name_line_column`` for the macro
+   *node*, or ``$variable_line_column`` if *variable* is given. Such names
+   cannot clash with the names of the program.
+
+.. function:: expand(tree, filename='<unknown>', macros=None)
+
+   Expand the macros in the AST *tree* (in place) and return it.
+   :func:`ast.parse` returns the tree *before* the expansion of macros;
+   this function can be used to look at the result of the expansion::
+
+      print(ast.unparse(macros.expand(ast.parse(source))))
+
+   *macros* is an optional dict mapping names to macro processors. These
+   macros can be used in *tree* without importing them, and the macros that
+   *tree* imports at its top level are added to the dict. The *macros*
+   argument of :func:`compile` works in the same way; it is used by
+   :mod:`codeop` to keep the macros imported in an interactive session.
+
+Statement expressions
+---------------------
+
+Macro processors can use the node :class:`ast.StmtExpr` to execute a statement
+in an expression: ``StmtExpr(stmt, value)`` executes *stmt* and then evaluates
+to *value*. The statement cannot use :keyword:`return`, :keyword:`break` or
+:keyword:`continue` to leave the expression.
+
+The nodes are also available under the names used by the PEP:
+``ast.macro_stmt``, ``ast.macro_expr`` and ``ast.stmt_expr``.
